<worksheet xml:id="sec-quiz-sampling-phat-att-2">
  <title>Quiz: Sampling Distribution of <m>\widehat{p}</m> Attempt 2</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Name: <fillin characters="45"/></p>

    <assemblage>
      <p><em>Sampling distribution formulas</em></p>
      <p>
        <m>\widehat{p}=\frac{x}{n}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>\mu_{\widehat{p}}=p</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>\sigma_{\widehat{p}}=\sqrt{\frac{p(1-p)}{n}}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>z=\frac{\widehat{p}-\mu_{\widehat{p}}}{\sigma_{\widehat{p}}}
        =\frac{\widehat{p}-p}{\sqrt{\frac{p(1-p)}{n}}}</m>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Show your setup or the R command used. Round probabilities to four decimal
      places. Round z-scores, standard deviations, and cutoff values to two
      decimal places.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-sampling-phat-att-2-model" workspace="1.25in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A researcher proposes four sampling plans for separate seed lots.
        Each plan uses a simple random sample without replacement. Here,
        <m>N</m> is the number of seeds in the lot, <m>n</m> is the sample size,
        and <m>p</m> is the population proportion of seeds that would germinate.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each plan, determine whether the conditions support using the
        usual normal model for <m>\widehat{p}</m>. Show the numerical checks
        and identify any condition that fails.
      </p>
      <ol marker="A.">
        <li><p><m>N=3{,}000,\quad n=100,\quad p=0.04</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>N=1{,}000,\quad n=200,\quad p=0.35</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>N=3{,}600,\quad n=180,\quad p=0.75</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>N=1{,}400,\quad n=140,\quad p=0.96</m></p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-sampling-phat-att-2-probabilities" workspace="1.25in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that 75% of the 20,000 seedlings in a nursery survive their
        first ten weeks. A simple random sample of 160 seedlings is selected
        without replacement. Let <m>\widehat{p}</m> be the proportion of
        sampled seedlings that survive. The conditions for the usual normal
        model are satisfied.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li><p>State the approximate shape, mean, and standard deviation of the sampling distribution of <m>\widehat{p}</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Find the probability that the sample proportion is within 0.05 (five percentage points) of the population proportion.</p></li>
        <li><p>Find the probability that the sample proportion differs from the population proportion by more than 0.05.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-sampling-phat-att-2-cutoff" workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that 85% of the 35,000 seeds in a storage lot are viable.
        A simple random sample of 350 seeds is selected without replacement.
        Let <m>\widehat{p}</m> be the proportion of sampled seeds that are viable.
      </p>
      <p>
        A sample is flagged for further investigation if its sample
        proportion falls in the lowest 20% of this sampling distribution.
        Find the cutoff proportion for flagging a sample and its corresponding
        <m>z</m>-score.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
